fter the women  drive off in the truck the movie ends  with Michael wandering off into a  clearing with the creepy looking  mannequins that Laurie uses for target  practice the script for this discarded  ending then states that Michael sits  down by a tree and exhausted his  breathing deepens it  his last much of this ending was  actually filmed as you can see various  scenes in the trailers and promo footage  which never made it into the final movie  and it can certainly see why it didn't  test so well with audiences as in a  number of ways it's not as satisfying as  the ending we did get firstly by placing  the ending outside we would have missed  out on Michael pushing Laurie off the  balcony in a nice reverse of how he fell  off in the original movie secondly  Laurie's achievement of finally trapping  Michael in the safe room would never  have happened making her character arc  not as fulfilling and thirdly in the  final film burning down the house also  gives her an additional sense of  resolution enclosure and on top of that  the deleted ending makes it more obvious  that Michael is very likely still alive  whereas in the final film it's left open  in a more interesting way a few fans  have said that even the ending we did  get felt a little abrupt  however this alternate ending sounds  like it would have been much shorter  again and perhaps rather anticlimactic a  major change that the Halloween 2018  filmmakers almost made the franchise's  mythos is that they very nearly altered  the ending of the first movie most  shockingly if the new filmmakers had  gone ahead with their alternate ending  to the original 1978 movie who would  have seen dr. Loomis killed on-screen  fortunately John Carpenter advised  director David Gordon Green not to do  that as in his opinion it would have  really annoyed the film's fans Green  Haqq thought that he needed to go back  40 years in his new film to remind  people about the ending to the first  movie in case they'd not seen it in a  long time or were new to the franchise  and he planned to use his art director  as a body double for Donald Pleasence as  dr. Loomis because apparently for the  kind of shot they were planning he would  have looked exactly like the long  deceased actor as for young Laurie she  would have been recreated using a blend  of Jamie Lee Curtis and a young actress  with a similar bill to a 19-year old  Curtis they also looked into digitally  altering existing footage from the  original movie and I wonder if they were  thinking of doing something similar to  what rogue one did with both Peter  Cushing's character and with a young  Princess Leia they were even going to  cast one of the movies production  assistants as a young sheriff Hawkins  because he looked like a young will  Patton who plays the present-day Hawkins  and that would have been filmed as part  of a scene showing Michael being  captured by the police in the end John  Carpenter told them they were making  things way too complicated and that the  audience didn't need this extra scene to  get up to speed so instead there was  just a single line of exposition from  dr. sauteing to Lori when he explains  that Hawkins was the first responding  deputy to arrive when Michael was  arrested in 1978 interestingly there's a  little remnant of this discarded reshoot  idea in the final film look carefully at  the room where Lori and Michael face off  in the finale and you'll notice that  it's a replica of the bedroom from the  end of the 1978 movie the filmmakers had  that set built to use for their initial  idea to reshoot the original film's  climax but when they decided against  that they used the set anyway to keep  down the budget and the several  additional deleted scenes that ended up  cut for pacing purposes the first one  comes just after Michael has escaped  from the prison bus we cut to a shot of  Dana the British podcaster taking a  shower the bathroom door opens slowly  and a masked man pulls back the s